<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tomochichi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1644–<a href="/years/1739">1739</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tomochichi was the leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamacraw">Yamacraw</a>, a small Creek-affiliated band living on a bluff above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">Savannah</a> River. He welcomed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-oglethorpe">James Oglethorpe</a> in 1733, allowing the founding of Savannah, and helped negotiate a treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-creek">Lower Creek</a>. In 1734 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> with Oglethorpe and met King George II. He remained an ally of the colony until his death in 1739.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tomochichi.jpg" alt="Tomochichi" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original uploader was Asarelah at en.wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomochichi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of Tomochichi's early life. He was presumably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> and was exiled from the Creek nation for reasons that are unclear. In about 1730 he formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamacraw">Yamacraw</a> from an assortment of Creek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamasee">Yamasee</a> people after the two nations disagreed over relations with the British and the Spanish. His group of about two hundred settled on the bluffs of the Savannah River, a burial place of his ancestors close to British traders. He had dealt with British colonists before and was cautious but unafraid of them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-oglethorpe">James Oglethorpe</a> and his settlers arrived in February 1733, Mary Musgrove, daughter of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> mother and an English father, and her husband John interpreted between the two men. Tomochichi chose to let them found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">Savannah</a> on Yamacraw Bluff for the sake of trade and diplomatic ties. Oglethorpe later recalled his first speech: he presented a buffalo skin painted with an eagle's head and feathers, the eagle signifying speed and love and the buffalo strength and protection, and asked the English to love and protect their little families.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1734 he gave King George II eagle feathers as a token of peace at Kensington Palace on 1 August, attended the ratification of the Articles of Friendship and Commerce, and pressed for education and fair trade. On his return he persuaded other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-creek">Lower Creek</a> chiefs to ally with the colony. He asked John Wesley for Christian teaching for his people without success, but Benjamin Ingham helped open an Indian school at Irene in September 1736. That year he joined Oglethorpe on an expedition to settle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>'s southern boundary and helped in dealings with the Spanish, and in February 1739 he met a Choctaw delegation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">Savannah</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Illness kept him from Oglethorpe's journey to Coweta in the summer of 1739, and he died on 5 October 1739, most accounts say at 95. He asked to be buried among the whites and was given a public military funeral with Oglethorpe as a pallbearer; his wife Senauki and nephew Toonahowi were left in charge of the tribe. The stone pyramid over his grave was last mentioned in 1759. A granite boulder was set up in his memory in 1899, and the Tomochichi Federal Building and U.S. Courthouse in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">Savannah</a> bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tomochichi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Oglethorpe founded Savannah in 1733, with the help of the Yamacraw leader <b>Tomochichi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-georgia">Province of Georgia</a></li></ul></section>
